{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/1349640-what-is-a-range-band","id":1349640,"title":"What is a range band?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-11-19T11:50:48-08:00","id":1349640,"title":"What is a range band?","content":"Hi folks!<br /> <br /> Been stumbling over these range bands in the 2e books in muliple places. What exactly are those?<br /> <br /> I can totally understand that a gun has medium range. But I dont't get sentences like &quot;you can move one (or sometimes even two) range bands&quot;. Can someone go to great lengths (meaning three range bands <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ) to explain that? Many thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3122-shuragil","name":"shuragil","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-19T12:03:32-08:00","id":1349644,"title":"","content":"They're semi-abstract ways of measuring distance. You have Close, Short, Medium, Long, and Extreme. If I'm in your face, I'm Close. I can move one range band away to be at Short range with you. If you are at Medium range from a gunman, and you both move one range band closer to each other, then you're at Close range.<br /> <br /> Positioning still matters. You can be Close with one NPC, Short with another, and Long with a third, but the first two might be in front of you and the third behind you. So, if you move one range band away from Close and Short NPCs, then you very well might be moving to Medium range with the Long NPC.<br /> <br /> Basically, it makes talking about distances much easier than having to try to measure everything out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","name":"Florin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"940"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-19T18:45:50-08:00","id":1349746,"title":"","content":"There's a full explanation on page 79-80 or so in the core rules, including a list of typical distances if you want more concrete numbers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1711-zeea","name":"Zeea","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}